\pard\b\fs20 The Beginning of the Churches\b0\par
\fs16\par
Before the year 0, Meduvia was the land of simple farmers who settled the plains between the sea and the western forest. To the north the fields reached into the mountains, and to the south to the great desert. The Meduvians knew nothing more of the great continent.\par
But one day something happened that was to change all of Meduvia forever...\par
On a stormy night, the hermit Barius woke from his sleep. When he stepped out of his hut, he saw that the sky was lit up by many lights. They all shot down onto the land. One great mountain drew especially many of these lights. That surely meant something. A sign!\par
The very next morning, Barius set out to walk to this mountain. There he found a great deal of a new kind of material that he had never seen before. That night he dreamed that Wuotan spoke to him. He had been chosen to proclaim the faith to the Meduvians.\par
Then, at the foot of this mountain, which was later known only by the name Ilureb, Barius built a great fortress: Telotiosus.\par
The sending of the Holigon and Barius's vision were fixed as the year 0, the beginning of the calendar.\par
So the first Meduvian religion came into being, worshipping the almighty creator Wuotan. The castle Telotiosus became the center of the faith and, in the year 50, the training ground for the protectors of the faith, the paladins. The Holigon that Wuotan had sent to earth made the paladins mighty warriors.\par
But they strove for ever more power. In the end the wrathful Teiwaz took advantage of this and gave them treacherous miracles that increased their power. Sawel\'f4 wanted to counter this and in turn granted miracles to the paladins.\par
This quarrel that arose between Teiwaz and Sawel\'f4, and the paladins' hunger for power, drove the united church further and further apart.\par
Barius, whom the Holigon had granted a very long life, always tried to preserve the unity of the church.\par
Until finally, in the year 80, the founder Barius mysteriously disappeared. Many legends tell that Wuotan took him to himself because he wanted to save him from the schism, and that he would send him back later, when Meduvia was ready, to lead Meduvia back to unity.\par
But with the disappearance of Barius, the church finally fell apart. Meduvia's peaceful time was over, and the great war of faith shook Meduvia.\par
